    chordthm.CPD $e |- ( ph -> ( ( C - P ) F ( D - P ) ) = _pi ) $.
    chordthm.Q $e |- ( ph -> Q e. CC ) $.
    chordthm.ABcirc $e |- ( ph -> ( abs ` ( A - Q ) ) =
                                  ( abs ` ( B - Q ) ) ) $.
    chordthm.ACcirc $e |- ( ph -> ( abs ` ( A - Q ) ) =
                                  ( abs ` ( C - Q ) ) ) $.
    chordthm.ADcirc $e |- ( ph -> ( abs ` ( A - Q ) ) =
                                  ( abs ` ( D - Q ) ) ) $.
    $( The intersecting chords theorem.

       If points A, B, C, and D lie on a circle (with center Q, say), and
       the point P is on the interior of the segments AB and CD, then the two
       products of lengths PA ` x. ` PB and PC ` x. ` PD are equal.

       The Euclidean plane is identified with the complex plane, and the fact
       that P is on AB and on CD is expressed by the hypothesis that the angles
       APB and CPD are equal to ` _pi ` .

       The result is proven by using ~ chordthmlem5 twice to show that PA
       ` x. ` PB and PC ` x. ` PD both equal BQ<HTML><sup>2</sup></HTML> ` - `
       PQ<HTML><sup>2</sup></HTML>.
       This is similar to the proof of the theorem given in Euclid's
       _Elements_, where it is Proposition III.35.
       This is Metamath 100 proof #55.  (Contributed by David
       Moews, 28-Feb-2017.) $)
